  "helpSearchHint": "Search help topics",
  "helpEmptySearchResult": "No results found",
  "helpStartSectionTitle": "Getting Started",
  "helpStartOnboardingTitle": "The screens you see on first launch",
  "helpStartOnboardingBody": "When you first open the app, it walks you through permissions like notifications, exact alarms, and display-over-other-apps. These are required for alarms to fire at the exact time even on the lock screen — skipping any of them can make alarms fire late or not at all.\n\nOnce every permission is granted, you'll move on to creating your work schedule.",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesTitle": "Repeating vs. irregular schedules",
  "helpStartScheduleTypesBody": "You can build your work schedule in two ways.\n\nA repeating schedule follows a fixed pattern — like \"day, day, night, night, off, off, rest\" — that keeps repeating from a chosen start date. Once you set the pattern and start date, the app fills in every future date automatically.\n\nAn irregular schedule has no fixed repeating pattern, so you assign a shift to each date yourself. Long-press a date on the calendar to assign a shift to that day or several days at once.",
  "helpShiftSectionTitle": "Managing Shifts",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignTitle": "Assigning shifts as an irregular worker",
  "helpShiftIrregularAssignBody": "If you're on an irregular schedule, long-press a date in the Calendar tab and choose which shift you're working that day (or several days at once). This applies only to those dates, not a repeating pattern — you set each date individually.\n\nIf you assign shifts a few days ahead of time, alarms for those days are prepared automatically based on the assignment.",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftTitle": "Changing the shift on a specific date",
  "helpShiftChangeShiftBody": "Whether you're on a repeating or irregular schedule, you can override a single date by long-pressing it on the calendar and picking a different shift. Alarms for that day are automatically recalculated to match the new shift.",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsTitle": "Customizing shift colors",
  "helpShiftShiftColorsBody": "Every calendar theme has default colors per shift, but you can override the color of a specific shift name if you like. Pick the shift name in the shift settings and choose a color — your choice takes priority over the theme's default.",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsTitle": "What is the \"all shifts\" view?",
  "helpShiftAllShiftsBody": "This view shows the shift pattern of coworkers on other teams at the same workplace — handy for checking things like \"when is Team A off this week?\"\n\nYou can open it from the Calendar tab. For it to show correctly, you need to set up your team structure first (how many teams there are, and how many days ahead or behind each one rotates relative to you).",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeTitle": "When your team changes",
  "helpShiftTeamChangeBody": "Go to Settings → Edit → Change Schedule and assign today's shift. Your shift names and alarms stay exactly as they are.",
  "helpAlarmSectionTitle": "Alarms",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesTitle": "Creating fixed alarms",
  "helpAlarmTemplatesBody": "You can register up to 5 alarms per shift. They can fire on the shift day itself, the day before, or the day after, and each alarm has its own time, sound, and other settings.",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeTitle": "Changing alarm sounds / volume issues",
  "helpAlarmSoundVolumeBody": "You can choose from 7 built-in alarm sounds or your device's own system alarm sounds. Speaker characteristics vary by device, so the app auto-corrects volume, but if it still feels off, try switching to a different sound in the alarm settings.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingTitle": "If an alarm doesn't go off",
  "helpAlarmAlarmNotRingingBody": "The most common cause is permissions. Check that \"exact alarms\", \"notifications\", and \"display over other apps\" are all enabled for this app, and that it's excluded from battery optimization.\n\nThe next most common cause is the shift or alarm setup itself — make sure a shift is actually assigned to that date, and that the shift has a fixed alarm registered. Assigning a shift without a fixed alarm means no alarm will fire.",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryTitle": "Viewing alarm history",
  "helpAlarmAlarmHistoryBody": "For an accurate alarm record, check Settings → Alarm History.",
  "helpCalendarSectionTitle": "Calendar & Home Widget",
  "helpCalendarThemesTitle": "Changing the calendar theme",
  "helpCalendarThemesBody": "You can choose from 10 calendar color themes. The choice applies not just inside the app's calendar but also to the home-screen widget.",
  "helpCalendarOtTitle": "Recording and checking overtime",
  "helpCalendarOtBody": "From a date's detail view in the Calendar tab you can log overtime for that day. What you log also feeds into the weekly and monthly work-hours totals.",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayTitle": "Checking weekly and monthly work hours",
  "helpCalendarWorkHoursPayBody": "Enter each shift's clock-in/out times in settings and the app automatically calculates your monthly and weekly work hours. Overtime is included, and you can choose in settings whether a shift change also applies automatically.",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetTitle": "Adding the home-screen widget",
  "helpCalendarHomeWidgetBody": "Long-press your home screen, open the widget picker, and add this app's calendar widget. It shows shift names and memos.",
  "helpScheduleTabSectionTitle": "Schedule Tab",
  "helpScheduleTabWhatIsItTitle": "How is the Schedule tab different?",
  "helpScheduleTabWhatIsItBody": "While a calendar memo is a note for the whole day, the Schedule tab lets you manage what you're doing from one specific time to another within a day. It's for things with an actual time attached, like an appointment before work or plans after your shift.",
  "helpScheduleTabCreateEditTitle": "Creating and editing a schedule item",
  "helpScheduleTabCreateEditBody": "Tap the shift-clock icon button on the right side of the screen to set a time, then tap it once more to bring up the schedule creation screen.",
  "helpScheduleTabAutoCategoryTitle": "How matching icons get picked",
  "helpScheduleTabAutoCategoryBody": "The app reads what you typed and automatically attaches a matching icon. If it can't find a good match, a default shape icon is attached instead.",
  "helpConditionSectionTitle": "Condition Manager",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItTitle": "What is the Condition tab?",
  "helpConditionWhatIsItBody": "This screen analyzes your work schedule and sleep records and shows that day's condition, along with a score, factoring in the sleep and fatigue load of shift work. Every judgment is grounded in actual sleep-medicine and occupational-health research.",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListTitle": "Condition judgment evidence",
  "helpConditionEvidenceListBody": "Every judgment and tip in the Condition tab cites at least one of these 13 evidence entries — nothing is judged without a cited source.\n\nEVIDENCE-001 · Shift rotation direction — Forward rotation (day→evening→night) is reported to be easier to adapt to than backward rotation. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-002 · Minimum rest between shifts — Recommends at least 11 hours of rest between two shifts. (Knauth & Hornberger, cited in NIOSH Module 5)\n\nEVIDENCE-003 · Minimum rest between shifts (legal standard) — Every worker must get at least 11 consecutive hours of rest per 24 hours. (EU Working Time Directive, Art. 3)\n\nEVIDENCE-004 · Long shifts and injury/accident risk — Relative risk is modeled at about +13% for a 10-hour shift and +27% for a 12-hour shift, versus an 8-hour shift. (Folkard & Lombardi, 2006)\n\nEVIDENCE-005 · Rest days after extended long shifts — Suggests at least 2 days of rest after 3 consecutive days of 12+ hour shifts. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-006 · Sleep after a night shift — Recommends prioritizing sleep after a night shift. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-007 · Napping before a night shift — Conditionally helpful, but the guideline itself states the evidence level is very low. Watch for grogginess right after waking. (AASM, 2025)\n\nEVIDENCE-008 · Caffeine and sleep — Caffeine taken even 6 hours before bedtime significantly cuts total sleep time by over an hour. (Drake et al., 2013)\n\nEVIDENCE-009 · Light exposure on the commute home after a night shift — Reducing bright light exposure can help later sleep (but safety comes first if you're too drowsy to drive safely). (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-010 · Eating during a night shift — Recommends eating less between midnight and 6am and favoring light meals. (NIOSH)\n\nEVIDENCE-011 · Recommended sleep for adults — Adults aged 18–64 are recommended 7–9 hours of sleep. (National Sleep Foundation, 2015)\n\nEVIDENCE-012 · Maximum weekly working hours (legal standard) — Average weekly working time, including overtime, must not exceed 48 hours. (EU Working Time Directive, Art. 6)\n\nEVIDENCE-013 · Consecutive night shifts and accident risk — Relative risk of accidents/injuries accumulates with each successive night shift (about +6% on night 2, +17% on night 3, +36% on night 4 — the increase is steeper for consecutive night shifts than for consecutive day/evening shifts). (Folkard & Tucker, 2003)",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededTitle": "Setup required before using this tab",
  "helpConditionSetupNeededBody": "You need to enter clock-in/out times for at least one shift name under Settings → Work Hours & OT first. Until you do, the Condition tab shows only a guidance message and no analysis. You don't need to fill in every shift — just one you're currently using is enough.",
  "helpSleepSectionTitle": "Sleep Tracking",
  "helpSleepWidgetTitle": "What is the sleep-tracking widget?",
  "helpSleepWidgetBody": "Add the widget to your home screen and tap the sleep button before bed, then the wake button after you get up, to log your sleep in a couple of taps. The Condition tab uses this sleep record together with your work schedule to predict your condition.",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectTitle": "Automatic sleep estimation",
  "helpSleepAutoDetectBody": "Even without touching the widget, the app can guess when you were probably asleep based on your work schedule and how long your phone went untouched. This is always just an estimate, though — you need to confirm it before it becomes an official record.",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarTitle": "Reviewing and editing recent sleep records",
  "helpSleepMiniCalendarBody": "The \"Recent Sleep\" mini calendar on the Condition tab shows confirmed records day by day. Tap a slot to create, edit, or delete a record right there. For older records, use \"View All\" to browse month by month.",
  "helpFriendSectionTitle": "Friend Sharing",
  "helpFriendWhatIsItTitle": "What is friend sharing?",
  "helpFriendWhatIsItBody": "This lets you share your work schedule so a friend or someone else can view it — or view a schedule someone else shared with you. You can share your schedule easily even with people who don't have the app.",
  "helpFriendShareCodeTitle": "Generating and sharing your code",
  "helpFriendShareCodeBody": "Open \"My Share Code\" from settings to generate a code. Give it to the other person, and they can register it in their own app to view your schedule.",
  "helpFriendViewFriendTitle": "Viewing a friend's schedule with their code",
  "helpFriendViewFriendBody": "If you've received a friend's code, add them from the friend list screen by entering it — their schedule then shows up as a calendar inside your own app.",
  "helpFriendReinstallNoteTitle": "What happens if you reinstall the app",
  "helpFriendReinstallNoteBody": "Reinstalling the app creates what's internally treated as a new account, which breaks any friend-sharing connections you had before. To reconnect, generate a new share code and send it to the other person again.\n\nYour friend won't see any error on their end — their screen will just keep showing your schedule exactly as it was right before you reinstalled. Since they have no way to notice this on their own, be sure to let them know if you reinstall.",
  "helpBackupSectionTitle": "Backup & Restore",
  "helpBackupDataStorageTitle": "Where is my data stored?",
  "helpBackupDataStorageBody": "Things like alarms, your work schedule, memos, schedule items, and sleep records are stored only on your own device. None of it is sent to an external server, only you can access it, and it's gone if you delete the app — which is exactly why backups matter.\n\nThe one exception is if you turn on friend sharing — in that case only the minimum needed to share (shift pattern, colors) is uploaded to a server (Firestore). Memos, overtime, work hours, and alarm settings are never uploaded, even then.",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupTitle": "When does automatic backup happen?",
  "helpBackupAutoBackupBody": "Whenever you leave the app (pressing home or switching apps), it backs up automatically — but only if something actually changed since the last backup. It's saved to the Download/ShiftBell folder on your device; it's not sent to the cloud or another device automatically. You can also back up manually anytime via \"Back Up Now\" in settings.",
  "helpBackupRestoreTitle": "Restoring after reinstalling or moving to a new device",
  "helpBackupRestoreBody": "After a fresh install, if a backup file exists on the device, the restore screen appears automatically on first launch. To move to a new device, first copy the backup file from the old device's Download/ShiftBell folder into the same folder on the new device, then install the app.\n\nAfter restoring, permissions like notifications and exact alarms are asked for again as a safety measure.",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualTitle": "If automatic restore doesn't find your backup",
  "helpBackupRestoreManualBody": "On some devices (certain Samsung models in particular), automatic detection can fail to find a backup that's actually there. Use the \"Load manually\" link on the restore screen to open a file picker and select the backup file from the Download/ShiftBell folder yourself.",
  "helpBackupRestoreOldBackupFailTitle": "A very old backup may fail to restore",
  "helpBackupRestoreOldBackupFailBody": "The data format the app saves occasionally changes as it's updated. If there's a big version gap between when a backup was made and when you restore it (for example, restoring a backup made a long time ago into a much newer version of the app), the restore can fail. Retrying with the same file will likely fail again for the same reason. To avoid this, it's a good idea to tap \"Back up now\" in Settings right before switching phones or reinstalling, to make a fresh backup — even if automatic backups have been working fine, doing one manually right before an important moment adds an extra layer of safety.",
  "helpTroubleshootSectionTitle": "Troubleshooting",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueTitle": "My alarm didn't go off",
  "helpTroubleshootAlarmIssueBody": "First check that notifications, exact alarms, and display-over-other-apps are enabled for this app, and that it's excluded from battery optimization. Then confirm a shift is actually assigned to that date and that the shift has a fixed alarm registered.",
  "helpTroubleshootForceStopTitle": "I force-stopped this app from phone settings",
  "helpTroubleshootForceStopBody": "\"Force stop\" from Settings → Apps cancels every alarm this app had scheduled. This is a much stronger action than just closing the app (going to the home screen) or swiping it away from recent apps - neither of those affects alarms at all.\n\nReopening the app after a force stop automatically re-registers any alarm whose time hasn't passed yet. However, an alarm whose time already passed between the force stop and the next time you reopen the app will not be restored and will not ring that day.\n\nSo if you've force-stopped the app, make sure to open it at least once before your next alarm is due - it will then ring normally again.",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueTitle": "The widget isn't updating",
  "helpTroubleshootWidgetIssueBody": "Try removing the widget and adding it again. If that doesn't help, open the app once and check again.",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingTitle": "I can't find my backup file",
  "helpTroubleshootBackupFileMissingBody": "Check the Download/ShiftBell folder directly using your device's file manager app. If the folder doesn't exist, no automatic backup has run yet — we recommend tapping \"Back Up Now\" in settings to create one.",
  "helpTroubleshootFriendDisconnectedTitle": "My friend's schedule hasn't changed in days",
  "helpTroubleshootFriendDisconnectedBody": "If the schedule just looks frozen with no error shown, it's almost always because your friend reinstalled the app. Reinstalling issues a new internal account, which breaks the existing connection — but nothing on screen tells you that happened, it just keeps showing whatever it last had.\n\nCheck the \"Last synced\" time at the top of your friend's schedule screen — if it's stuck on an old date, that's the sign. Ask your friend for a new share code, then remove the old entry and add them again with it.",

